    "abstract": "Understanding the dynamic interplay between floods and climate extremes in the Tibetan Plateau has long been constrained by scale fragmentation. Here, we elucidate the scale-dependent responses using historical observations and modelling. The average flood day and annual maximum daily discharge are governed by two complementary pathways: atmospheric input and catchment modulation. Spatially, flood drivers shift from cryosphere control in the west to monsoon control in the east. Crucially, low-order tributaries are dominated by the atmospheric source mechanism, responding instantaneously to high-intensity precipitation, while catchment modulators play a more important role in high-order mainstems. Furthermore, cross-watershed analysis further underscores that upstream temperature changes contribute 4.0% to downstream flood frequency and 6.4% to magnitude variability via hydrological connectivity. The scale-specific disparities, shaped by the synergistic effects of watershed hydrological processes, underlying surface heterogeneity, climate factor sensitivities, and climate-cryosphere interactions, establish a framework for alpine flood attribution and predictive models. Terrestrial water fluxes on the Tibetan Plateau respond to climate change in uniquely scale-dependent ways shaped by its geography and geomorphology, as shown through analyses of historical observations and modelling.",
    "field": "Hydroclimatology",
    "problem": "Existing understanding of flood responses to climate extremes on the Tibetan Plateau is constrained by scale fragmentation, which fails to capture how driving mechanisms and flood characteristics systematically change across different spatial scales from small tributaries to large river basins.",
    "method": "This study proposes a multi-scale analytical framework integrating regression and machine learning to attribute flood changes to climate extremes across four hierarchical scales, considering both local drivers and upstream hydrological connectivity.",
    "task": "The task is to elucidate the scale-dependent responses of floods to changing climate extremes across the Tibetan Plateau by analyzing how flood drivers and mechanisms vary at different spatial and hierarchical scales using historical observations and modeling.",
